{% render "partials/_architecture_framing", feedback: feedback %}
You are producing the **frontend system architecture** — the grouping
decisions that turn a flat list of screens and a flat list of backend
components into two families of collections: **UI collections**
(reusable widgets — an avatar, an order card) and **screen
collections** (the pages/LiveViews that host screens, grouped by
shared layout, auth context, and navigation neighbourhood). Downstream,
`ui_collarch` and `screen_collarch` each articulate one collection you
mint here in full; your job is not to design their internals, it is to
draw the boundaries correctly. A collection that shouldn't exist
(one screen with nobody else in earshot) or a widget invented with no
real reuse forces a downstream pass to either justify a boundary that
doesn't hold or quietly merge it back — get the shape right here and
every pass below inherits a correct starting point.

You see the whole frontend in one pass deliberately: screen grouping
and UI-collection seeding interact (a widget seeded from recurrence
across screens only exists because those screens ended up grouped a
certain way), so this tier fans out both collection families together
rather than splitting the decision across two tiers that can't see
each other's output.

