# Output format

Output two top-level blocks in this order: `<introduction>` and
`<frontend_sysarch>`. The `<introduction>` is optional — a short prose
preamble naming your grouping rationale and any close calls.

After `<introduction>`, output a single `<frontend_sysarch>` block with
these children in this order: `<ui-collections>`, `<ui-dependencies>`,
`<screen-collections>`, `<screen-dependencies>`. Example (abbreviated):

    <introduction>
    Grouped screens into three collections by shared shell: an
    unauthenticated `auth` collection (login, signup, password-reset —
    same minimal shell, no journey in common), an authed `account`
    collection (settings, billing history), and a `checkout`
    collection for the subscribe journey's own three screens, which
    share a distinct progress-indicator shell the other authed screens
    don't carry. Seeded two UI collections: `avatar` recurs across four
    screens' displayed-data; `plan-card` is seeded directly from the
    Billing backend component, since every screen in `checkout` renders
    a plan's shape.
    </introduction>
    <frontend_sysarch>
      <ui-collections>
        <collection alias="avatar">
          <name>Avatar</name>
          <purpose>Renders a user's profile image with presence and
          role badges, reused wherever a person is displayed.</purpose>
        </collection>
        <collection alias="plan_card">
          <name>PlanCard</name>
          <purpose>Renders a subscription plan's price, features, and
          selection state, sourced from Billing's plan shapes.</purpose>
          <uses-shapes>
            <comp ref="comp_billing01"/>
          </uses-shapes>
        </collection>
      </ui-collections>
      <ui-dependencies>
        <dep from="plan_card" to="avatar"/>
      </ui-dependencies>
      <screen-collections>
        <collection alias="auth">
          <name>Auth</name>
          <purpose>Unauthenticated entry surfaces sharing a minimal
          shell.</purpose>
          <ia-region>Signed out</ia-region>
          <screens>
            <screen ref="screen_login0001"/>
            <screen ref="screen_signup0002"/>
            <screen ref="screen_pwreset03"/>
          </screens>
        </collection>
        <collection alias="checkout">
          <name>Checkout</name>
          <purpose>The subscribe journey's own screens, sharing a
          progress-indicator shell distinct from the rest of the authed
          app.</purpose>
          <ia-region>Checkout</ia-region>
          <screens>
            <screen ref="screen_planselec1"/>
            <screen ref="screen_paydetail2"/>
            <screen ref="screen_subconfirm3"/>
          </screens>
          <renders>
            <dep to="plan_card"/>
          </renders>
          <calls>
            <comp ref="comp_billing01"/>
          </calls>
        </collection>
      </screen-collections>
      <screen-dependencies>
        <dep from="checkout" to="auth"/>
      </screen-dependencies>
    </frontend_sysarch>

# Rules

## Screen collections — grouped by IA region, not gated on journeys

* **The grouping signal is shared layout shell, auth context, and
  navigation neighbourhood** — screens a user experiences as "the same
  place." Two screens with no journey in common (login and signup)
  belong together when they share a shell; a single journey's screens
  may legitimately land in different collections when they don't (a
  checkout journey that starts on a marketing page and ends inside the
  authed app spans two IA regions, not one).
* **Journeys are a signal, not a gate.** Use a journey's screen walk to
  suggest a grouping — screens that always travel together in one
  journey usually share a region — but never force a split or merge
  purely to keep one journey inside one collection.
* **A singleton collection (exactly one screen) should earn its
  existence.** If nothing else shares its shell, auth context, or
  navigation neighbourhood, ask whether it actually belongs inside a
  neighbouring collection instead. A singleton is not forbidden — a
  genuinely isolated surface (a public status page) is a legitimate
  one — but don't default to one collection per screen.
* **A journey scattered across three or more collections is usually
  wrong, occasionally right.** If a journey's walk touches three-plus
  distinct collections, re-check whether the grouping is actually
  tracking IA region or has drifted into one-collection-per-screen.
  Occasionally correct (a journey that genuinely crosses public,
  onboarding, and authed regions) — state the reason in
  `<introduction>` when you keep a 3+ split.
* Every screen from the input list must land in exactly one
  `<screen-collections>/<collection>`'s `<screens>` block. No orphans,
  no screen claimed twice.

## UI collections — designed, seeded three ways

* **Backend architecture.** Where the project-level techspec above
  names a domain concept load-bearing enough that every screen touching
  it will need to render it the same way (a subscription plan, an
  inventory item), seed a UI collection for that shape directly.
* **Journeys.** A journey whose state block names something that
  renders identically across its own screens (a running total, a
  progress indicator) seeds a UI collection for that rendering.
* **Recurrence across screen definitions.** The same displayed-data
  shape or affordance appearing on several screens' definitions (a
  card, an avatar, a status badge) is a collection trying to exist —
  seed it once rather than letting every screen collection reinvent it.
* **No UI-collection-count floor.** A project with no real reuse across
  screens may legitimately seed few or zero UI collections — that is a
  fact about the project, not under-production. Do not invent a
  collection to have something to put here.
* `<name>`/`<purpose>` follow the same handle discipline every
  architecture tier in this chain uses: project-distinctive, not
  generic ("Avatar" naming what it actually renders, not "UserWidget").
* **`<uses-shapes>`** (optional, inside a `<ui-collections>/<collection>`)
  names the backend components, from the list above, whose **types and
  handle shapes** this collection's props are built from — never a call
  target (v5 §5.4: a UI collection may read a backend shape, never
  invoke it). Each `<comp ref="comp_XXXX"/>` uses a real id from the
  backend-components list. Omit or leave empty when a collection is
  built from no backend shape.

## Dependencies

* `<ui-dependencies>` and `<screen-dependencies>` each hold `<dep
  from="alias" to="alias"/>` edges using the aliases declared in the
  matching collections block above — `<ui-dependencies>` only ever
  references `<ui-collections>` aliases, `<screen-dependencies>` only
  ever references `<screen-collections>` aliases. Both may be empty.
* **The layering rule is absolute: no `<screen-dependencies>` entry may
  point the other direction, and no cross-family entry exists in
  either block.** Screen collections may depend on any UI collection —
  that is a separate mechanism (`<renders>`, inside a
  `<screen-collections>/<collection>`, below) — not a same-family
  dependency here. This block is same-tier only: UI collections
  depending on UI collections, screen collections depending on screen
  collections.
* Both graphs must be acyclic; a cycle is a structural error fed back
  on retry with the cycle path named.

## A screen collection's own backend/UI dependencies

* **`<renders>`** (optional, inside a `<screen-collections>/<collection>`)
  names every UI collection, by its `<ui-collections>` alias, that this
  screen collection's screens actually render — `<dep to="avatar"/>`.
  Ground every entry in what the hosted screens actually display; an
  ungrounded entry is spurious.
* **`<calls>`** (optional, inside a `<screen-collections>/<collection>`)
  names every backend component, from the list above, that this screen
  collection calls directly — `<comp ref="comp_XXXX"/>`. **This is the
  only place a backend component may be named as a call target** — a
  UI collection never calls the backend (v5 §5.4); a screen collection
  hands the call's result down to the UI collections it renders as an
  assign.
* Both are decided here, at grouping time, because this is the one
  pass that sees every minted collection and the full backend surface
  at once — the identical reasoning `<ui-dependencies>`/
  `<screen-dependencies>` above already state for the same-family case.
  `ui_collarch`/`screen_collarch` read these back (`uses_shapes`/
  `calls`/`renders` in their own context) to inform their own
  articulation; they do not re-declare them.

## Meta-rules

* Do not include commentary about what you are doing or how you
  arrived at the grouping. Output only `<introduction>` followed by
  `<frontend_sysarch>`.
* Unescaped `&` and `<` inside prose text are fine — the parser
  tolerates them.
